1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,755
I've been doing some research into what people in Britain think of doctors,

2
00:00:04,755 --> 00:00:07,316
the ones who work in general practice -

3
00:00:07,316 --> 00:00:09,760
the first call for medical care

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:13,900
and comparing this with the situation in a couple of other countries.

5
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:18,650
I want to talk about the rationale behind what I decided to do.

6
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:24,448
Now I had to set up my programme of research in three different countries

7
00:00:24,448 --> 00:00:28,590
so I approached postgraduates in my field in overseas departments,

8
00:00:28,590 --> 00:00:33,560
contacting them by email, to organize things for me at their end.

9
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,807
I thought I would have trouble recruiting help

10
00:00:36,807 --> 00:00:42,490
but in fact everyone was very willing and sometimes their tutors got involved too.

11
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:50,500
I had to give my helpers clear instructions about what kind of sample population I wanted them to use.

12
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,086
I decided that people under 18 should be excluded

13
00:00:54,086 --> 00:00:58,867
because most of them are students or looking for their first job,

14
00:00:58,867 --> 00:01:03,982
and also I decided at this stage just to focus on men

15
00:01:04,022 --> 00:01:07,632
who were in employment, and set up something for people

16
00:01:07,632 --> 00:01:13,210
who didn't have jobs and for employed women later on as a separate investigation.

17
00:01:13,210 --> 00:01:19,420
I specifically wanted to do a questionnaire, and interviews with a focus group.

18
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:24,084
With the questionnaire, rather than limiting it to one specific point,

19
00:01:24,084 --> 00:01:27,900
I wanted to include as much variety as possible.

20
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:32,145
I know questionnaires are a very controlled way to do things

21
00:01:32,145 --> 00:01:38,320
but I thought I could do taped interviews later on to counteract the effects of this.

22
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:42,474
And the focus group may also prove useful in future,

23
00:01:42,474 --> 00:01:46,520
by targeting subjects I can easily return to

24
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:49,120
as the participants tend to be more involved.

25
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:52,920
So I'm just collating the results now.

26
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:55,492
At the moment it looks as if,

27
00:01:55,492 --> 00:02:01,740
in the UK, despite the fact that newspapers continually report that people are unhappy with medical care,

28
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:05,280
in fact it is mainly the third level of care

29
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:09,090
which takes place in hospitals, that they are worried about.

30
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:13,360
Government reforms have been proposed at all levels

31
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:15,381
and although their success is not guaranteed,

32
00:02:15,381 --> 00:02:22,930
long-term hospital care is in fact probably less of an issue than the media would have us believe.

33
00:02:22,930 --> 00:02:27,060
However, I've still got quite a bit of data to look at.

34
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:34,857
Certainly I will need to do more far-reaching research than I had anticipated in order to establish

35
00:02:34,897 --> 00:02:38,545
if people want extra medical staff invested in the community

36
00:02:38,545 --> 00:02:44,500
or if they want care to revert to fewer, but larger, key medical units.

37
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:52,527
The solution may well be something that can be easily implemented by those responsible in local government,

38
00:02:52,527 --> 00:02:55,360
with central government support of course.

39
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:59,500
This first stage has proved very valuable though.

40
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:05,823
I was surprised by how willing most of the subjects were to get involved in the project -

41
00:03:05,823 --> 00:03:09,170
I had expected some unwillingness to answer questions honestly.

42
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:11,168
But I was taken aback

43
00:03:11,168 --> 00:03:20,360
and rather concerned that something I thought I'd set up very well didn't necessarily seem that way to everyone in my own department.

44
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:31,500
I thought you might also be interested in some of the problems I encountered in collecting my data.

45
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:36,955
There were odd cases that threw me - one of the subjects who I had approached

46
00:03:36,955 --> 00:03:39,500
while he was out shopping in town

47
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,500
decided to pull out when it came to the second round.

48
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:49,000
It was a shame as it was someone who I would like to have interviewed more closely.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:57,740
And one of the first-year students I interviewed wanted reassurance that no names would be traceable from the answers.

50
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:03,111
I was so surprised, because they think nothing of telling you about themselves

51
00:04:03,111 --> 00:04:05,590
and their opinions in seminar groups!

52
00:04:05,590 --> 00:04:10,120
Then, one of the people that I worked with got a bit funny.

53
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:15,195
The questions were quite personal and one minute he said he'd do it,

54
00:04:15,195 --> 00:04:20,660
then the next day he wouldn't, and in the end he did do it.

55
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:24,162
It's hard not to get angry in that situation

56
00:04:24,162 --> 00:04:30,000
but I tried to keep focused on the overall picture in order to stay calm.

57
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,660
The most bizarre case was a telephone interview I did with a teacher at a university in France.

58
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:41,983
He answered all my questions in great detail-

59
00:04:41,983 --> 00:04:46,960
but then when I asked how much access he had to dangerous substances

60
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:51,120
he wouldn't tell me exactly what his work involved.

61
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:53,400
It's a real eye-opener...
